Dark launching: Deploy feature to production but keep hidden from users (behind feature flag). Code exists, executes, but doesn't affect user experience. vs Canary: visible to subset of users. Dark launch use cases: 1) Load testing in production, 2) Gradual infrastructure provisioning, 3) Testing integrations without user impact, 4) Parallel run for comparison. Implementation:

@Service
public class OrderService {
  @Autowired FeatureFlags flags;
  @Autowired NewOrderProcessor newOrderProcessor;
  @Autowired LegacyOrderProcessor legacyOrderProcessor;
  @Autowired MetricsService metrics;

  public OrderResponse processOrder(OrderRequest request, User user) {
    // Production path
    OrderResponse response = legacyOrderProcessor.process(request);

    // Dark launch: new processor runs but result discarded
    if (flags.isDarkLaunchEnabled("new-order-processor", user)) {
      CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
        try {
          long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
          OrderResponse darkResponse = newOrderProcessor.process(request);
          long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;

          // Log for comparison
          metrics.record("order.dark_launch.duration", duration);
          metrics.record("order.dark_launch.success", 1);

          // Compare responses
          if (!darkResponse.equals(response)) {
            log.warn("Dark launch discrepancy detected: order={}, legacy={}, new={}", 
              request.getOrderId(), response, darkResponse);
          }
        } catch (Exception e) {
          metrics.record("order.dark_launch.error", 1);
          log.error("Dark launch error: ", e);
        }
      });
    }

    return response; // always return legacy response
  }
}

Feature flag configuration:
features:
  dark-launches:
    new-order-processor:
      enabled: true
      percentage: 10  # 10% of requests
      compareResults: true
      logDiscrepancies: true

Benefits:
- Zero user impact (errors don't affect production)
- Real production load testing
- Performance comparison under realistic conditions
- Gradual confidence building

Example dark launch progression:
Week 1: 10% dark launch, monitor errors and performance
Week 2: 50% dark launch, verify scalability
Week 3: 100% dark launch, compare all results
Week 4: Canary deployment (5% users see new results)
Week 5: Full rollout

Monitoring:
- Error rate comparison: legacy vs dark
- Performance comparison: latency, throughput
- Result discrepancy rate
- Resource usage (CPU, memory)

Difference summary:
Dark Launch:
- Code runs in production
- Results discarded
- Zero user impact
- Used for validation

Canary:
- Code runs in production
- Results returned to users
- Limited user impact
- Used for gradual rollout

Dark launching reduces risk before canary deployment. Provides production validation without stakes.
